{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793033984,"nonce":"bCcqcBkE96c199hKnUt6f5+dUuc=","title":"Fence document restore selection with filesystem mutation history","message":"Automatic compute restoration cannot safely select saved/journal records while browser filesystem operations bypass recovery storage. Implement a bounded ordered filesystem intent ledger, promote only compute-confirmed Save/journal heads atomically with their records, and expose an owner-checked source-scoped restore catalog. Remove/rename/write/mkdir invalidations must suppress older matching heads; pending or uncertain outcomes must fail closed. Retry responses must not lose mutation history. Keep user RPCs nonblocking and in the same bounded FIFO as document recovery.\n\nNative direct writes must update an open CRDT document before acknowledgement; removing a shared document or parent must be refused until it closes. Validate ordering, prefix boundaries, same-content barriers, failed/time-out operations, object restart, source/account isolation and record/metadata limits. Rebuild WASM/native session and restart the local service after the milestone.\n\nThis is restore selection and fencing, not automatic VM recovery. External filesystem changes and full filesystem durability, provider leases/fencing, stable identities, snapshot materialization and MSB production provisioning remain under 52138fcda83502ec87679713955f7cf7c7f7e8654a0ef55ba60357bd0f22a904 and 670bd15d5f2a4c8fdd82da62e9b72599f9a689affc5682a7b621f81fd72156c0. Plans must explicitly identify their partial scope so an operator cannot treat document recovery as a full workspace snapshot.","files":null}]}